course: religion-ethics
lesson: 77
title: Kindness to the dying and visiting the old
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Ellie visits her great-aunt in a hospice, a calm place that cares for people who are dying, and worries «what do I say? she can hardly talk»; but she does not need many words: holding a hand, smiling, reading aloud or showing a drawing can mean a lot, and hearing is thought to be one of the last senses to fade.
explain: Dying people are still people who enjoy ordinary things: Jasmine asks Lucy to paint her nails, and Sam's grandad would love to talk about football; families like Ethan's take turns to sit with Grandma so she is never alone.
explain: Old people in care homes and those living alone can be lonely: Oscar finds visiting his nan boring, but his visit may be the best part of her week, and Mia's gran telling the same story again is a chance to listen kindly.
explain: Jesus said, «I was sick and you looked after me, I was in prison and you visited me», and Christians see visiting the sick and old as serving him; the modern hospice was started by Cicely Saunders, a Christian nurse and doctor, in London in 1967, and people of every faith and none now support hospices.
explain: Kindness also means noticing: if a lonely neighbour has not been seen for days, tell a grown-up, who can check safely; an old person who has fallen may need help fast.
explain: For a child today this means: your company is a real gift, and being there is often enough.
fact: A hospice is a place that cares for people who are dying, helping them to be comfortable and live as well as they can.
fact: The modern hospice movement was started by Cicely Saunders, a Christian nurse and doctor, who opened St Christopher's Hospice in London in 1967.
fact: Hearing is thought to be one of the last senses to fade, so dying people may still hear kind words.
fact: Holding a hand, smiling, reading aloud or showing a drawing can comfort someone who cannot talk much.
fact: Many families take turns to sit with a dying relative so that they are never alone; Christians, Muslims, Jews, Hindus and others all have this tradition.
fact: Music can bring back strong memories; tears during a song do not always mean sadness.
fact: When writing to someone who will not get better, words like «I love you» or «Thinking of you» are both kind and true.
fact: Dying people still enjoy ordinary things, like having their nails painted or chatting about football.
fact: Old people living alone can fall or become ill; if one has not been seen for days, tell a grown-up straight away.
fact: Some old people forget they have told a story before; listening patiently is a kindness.
fact: Jesus taught that visiting the sick and those in prison is like visiting him.
fact: Children can join church or community groups that visit lonely old people, with a grown-up.
exercise: What is a hospice? | A place that cares for people who are dying | A hotel for people on holiday | A school for training new doctors | A shop that sells medicine
exercise: Ellie's great-aunt can hardly talk. What can Ellie do? | Hold her hand, smile or read to her | Stay away until she can talk again | Talk loudly to wake her up | Leave quickly so as not to bother her
exercise: Why might kind words still matter to someone very weak? | Hearing is thought to be one of the last senses to fade | Weak people hear better than anyone else | Kind words are a medicine that cures | They don't matter because nobody can hear
exercise: Who started the modern hospice movement? | Cicely Saunders, a Christian nurse and doctor | Florence Nightingale, in the Crimean War, at her army hospital | Queen Victoria, in Buckingham Palace | Alexander Fleming, who found penicillin
exercise: A man cries during «Silent Night» when Tom's class sings carols. Did they make him sad? | Not necessarily; music can bring back moving memories | Yes; singing carols to old people is unkind | Yes; he hated the song and wanted them to stop singing it at once | No; he had something in his eye
exercise: Lucy's grandad won't get better. What can she write on his card instead of «Get well soon»? | «I love you» or «Thinking of you» | «Goodbye for ever, my dear Grandad» | «Hurry up and get better, please, Grandad» | Nothing; she shouldn't send one at all
exercise: Why does Ethan's family take turns sitting with Grandma? | So she is never alone and feels safe and loved | To make sure she takes her pills | Because the hospice has no nurses | To guard her things from robbers
exercise: Holly's old neighbour who lives alone hasn't been seen for days. What should Holly do? | Tell a grown-up straight away | Wait another week to see | Climb in through a window | Do nothing; it's not her business
exercise: Oscar thinks visiting Nan in the care home is boring. What's true? | His visit may be the best part of her week | Nan doesn't really notice who comes to visit her | Visits only matter at Christmas and birthdays | Old people prefer to be left alone in peace
exercise: What could Oscar bring to make visits better? | A game, a drawing or news about school | His homework, to do quietly in the corner | A phone to play on the whole time he is there, so he isn't bored | Nothing; it's best to sit in silence
exercise: Mia's gran tells the same story every visit. What is kind to do? | Listen patiently; she may have forgotten | Tell her she's said it a hundred times | Leave as soon as the story starts | Correct every single detail she gets wrong, so she remembers it right
exercise: Jasmine in the hospice asks Lucy to paint her nails. Why? | Dying people still enjoy ordinary, fun things | Hospices make everyone have painted nails | She wants to look nice for her funeral | It is a hospice rule for every visitor
exercise: Can Sam talk to his dying grandad about football? | Yes; normal chat helps him feel part of life | No; you must only talk about death and serious things | No; it is rude to mention sport to someone so ill | Only if they both support the very same football team
exercise: What did Jesus say about visiting the sick? | It is like visiting him | It should be left to doctors | It is only for priests | It is a waste of time
exercise: Freddie's church visits lonely old people. Can kids help? | Yes, with a grown-up | No, only adults allowed | Only on Sundays at church | Only if they are 18
exercise: Which of these traditions stay with the dying so they're not alone? | Christian, Muslim, Jewish, Hindu and others | Only Christian families do this, because it is in the Bible | Only Buddhist monks do this | No tradition does this any more
test: «She can't even talk, what's the point me goin',» says Ellie's cousin in Manchester. What's the best answer? | Being there, holding her hand, still means a lot | There's no point at all if she can't talk back to you | She'll be too asleep to notice anyone | Only grown-ups' visits count
test: «Nan's care home is so boring, like, peak boredom, do I HAVE to go?» asks Oscar (peak = the worst). What's the wisest reply? | Your visit may be the best bit of Nan's week | No, stay home; Nan won't mind or even notice you're not there | Go, but don't talk to her | Only go when there's cake
test: The vicar announces, very formally: «Volunteers are sought to accompany our housebound members on Thursday afternoons.» What is he asking for? | People to visit old church members who can't go out | People to clean the church on Thursdays | People to drive the vicar to the shops | People to stay home on Thursdays
test: What would happen if nobody checked on an old neighbour who'd had a fall? | She might lie hurt for days without help | She would always get up on her own | Someone would ring her eventually anyway | Nothing; falls are never serious
test: «Grandad's dying, innit, so writin' 'get well soon' is a lie, yeah?» asks Lucy in East London. What could she write instead? | «I love you» or «Thinking of you» | «See you never, Grandad, bye-bye now» | Nothing at all, just a blank card with a stamp | «Get well» anyway, just to be polite
test: A rough cousin moans, «Same bloody story again, Gran, we've heard it.» What's kinder? | Listen patiently; it makes her happy | Agree with him and walk out of the room | Tell her she's losing it and needs help | Correct her and then start another story
test: «Sittin' with Gran all night when she's asleep? That's a reet waste,» says a boy in Lancashire (reet = right/real). Why do families do it? | So she's never alone and may sense someone near | Because nurses don't work at night in hospices | To make sure nobody steals her things | Because it's the law in hospices that someone must sit with every patient all night
test: In a film, children run screaming from a care home because «old people are creepy». What's the real lesson? | Old people are people who love visits and chats | Care homes really are scary places, like in the film | Old people don't like children at all | Children should never visit care homes
test: «Nail polish? In a hospice? That's random,» says a boy in Vancouver. Why does Jasmine want it? | Dying people still enjoy normal, fun things | It's a kind of medicine painted on the nails | It is a hospice rule for everyone who stays there | It shows the nurses which patient is dying next
test: «My koro's in the rest home, I'll take him a footy card, sweet as,» says a boy in Wellington (koro = grandad, sweet as = great). Good idea? | Yes; sharing what you love helps him feel part of life | No; old people hate football | No; you should only bring flowers to a rest home, never cards or games | Only if the card is worth money
test: Myth to check: «A dying person doesn't care about visitors any more.» | False; company and kindness still matter a lot | True; they want to be left alone, in peace and quiet | True, because by then they cannot hear anyone at all | False, but only if the visitors bring nice gifts
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the purpose of a hospice? | The care and comfort of persons who are dying, that they may live as well as possible | The treatment of minor injuries sustained in sport | The accommodation of travellers upon holiday | The training of surgeons in new procedures
exam: Identify the person principally credited with founding the modern hospice movement. | Cicely Saunders | Florence Nightingale | Mary Seacole | Edith Cavell
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning an elderly neighbour who has not been seen for several days. | The matter ought to be reported to a responsible adult without delay | The matter ought to be disregarded as being of no concern | Entry ought to be forced by the child alone | The neighbour ought to be awaited for a further fortnight
exam: Whereas a visitor is uncertain what to say to a dying relative who cannot speak, ascertain the conduct most consistent with the matters taught. | Presence, the holding of a hand, and reading aloud | Prompt departure so as to avoid any disturbance to the patient or to the staff | Loud speech intended to rouse the patient | Discussion confined to the disposal of property
